nts so there were nerve gas  antidotes there was millions of vials of  smallpox vaccine  anthrax vaccines so a lot of medications  in cold storage eventually antiviral  medications in preparation for pandemic  influenza  there also were non non pharmaceutical  supplies for example and there's been a  lot of discussion of this ventilators  protective equipment that kind of thing  when it comes to the ventilator I don't  even really want to call it a debate but  what was for a while  fear of a shortage and what I would  characterize is the federal government's  slow implementation of distributing I  mean I think that some of those  ventilators could have been distributed  far earlier at very little cost if they  end up not being necessary okay you send  them back what else sort of should flesh  out our understanding of the ventilator  piece specifically I mean in terms of  certainly creating new ones we know that  the manufacturing process is not you  have them in 48 hours we're kind of now  building up a supply which will be  manufactured over the next three months  but what what insight do you have into  the entire ventilator situation yeah the  I guess the to step back one to step  back a little bit the important thing to  keep in mind also is the process of  distribution so a lot of planning and  thought went into you know once you have  supplies available how do you decide  where they should go how do you get them  to the right places how do you keep  track of how many supplies of a given  item you have and where they are among  this the various stockpiles in different  parts of the country so one piece of the  story is of course do you have enough of  them and that's the question you asked  but another is how do you get them to  the places where they're needed yes and  and when it comes to that is it  unrealistic of me to think that in late  January or early February there were  likely the signals that would have  supported starting to deploy them to  urban areas in some way or is it  unrealistic to think that that would  have been an appropriate time to do it  to be proactive yeah an important part  of stockpile planning is to put in place  the stockpiles of items where they're  needed as soon as you get a signal that  there might be a disaster on the way so  that's right as the World Health  was putting every nation in the world on  alert in late January as officials were  beginning to counsel the administration  that there might be an epidemic of a  very dangerous disease on the way in  particular respiratory disease indeed  that would have been the time to start  figuring out whether we had enough  ventilators and whether they were in the  right place so one more thing on the  stockpile thing and then I want to move  on to a few other things Jared Kushner  had this very strange Orwellian moment  where he said the stockpile is our  stockpile it's not the state stockpile  what I didn't that I don't even know  what to make of that in the sense that  well everybody I guess unless you're in  Guam or Puerto Rico you're in one of the  states like at we are the states but is  there anything about the stockpiles  origin that makes that statement less  Orwellian well it's a very confused  statement it doesn't understand the  whole premise of how governmental  preparedness has to work in a federal  system that like the United States the  whole presumption of a system that is  run by the federal government is that  states would make requests of the  federal government for the items when  they need them and that there would be a  elaborate and sophisticated coordination  between the federal government and the  states so it's not a competition between  the federal government and the states  it's not that it's belongs to the  federal government or us whatever Jared  Kushner meant by us but rather the real  effort is to figure out what the states  need and how can we get that material to  them yeah elaborate and sophisticated is  not 