ially combined with widespread  diagnostic testing that can do a decent  job of mitigating the disease threat I'm  obviously they can't cure it but so so  in certain countries that really have  thought carefully about how to do this  and have strong health systems in place  where it looks not so catastrophic um in  other parts of the world where you don't  have competent government government  leadership you don't have a really  strong health system you have a lot of  economic inequalities that's where we  see the catastrophic event unfolding one  of the things I've been pointing out is  that the virus has exposed the  underlying fragility of a lot of the  systems that sometimes feel very very  solid and you know we could find two  three months ago we could have found a  number of indicators of the American  economy that weren't great right I mean  huge inequality and wages have been  stagnant etc but by the traditional  measures the economy was humming along  quite well high consumer confidence low  unemployment high stock market etc the  fragility that is exposed by a complete  hundred and eighty degree turn around in  just a few weeks for me has been one of  the most stunning things and I don't  know that you know the the system we  have doesn't reward  Proactive it's not that profitable to be  proactive but that's been a huge thing  for me yeah no I completely agree not  only is the the health systems for  fragile the supply chain system is  fragile the security of what we call  essential workers is precarious one  thing that preparedness planners didn't  think a lot about was the how to deal  with the economic after effects of these  non-pharmaceutical interventions like  social distancing measures so I think  countries that have a much more  resilient economic system in place have  turned out to be much better prepared  than countries like ours that really  focused on efficiencies at the expense  of resilience that's interesting what we  know from a medical perspective with  that certain countries were far better  prepared South Korea certainly comes to  mind but what which are the countries  that economically were much better  prepared well I think country I would  say that countries in which it was taken  for granted that you would you would  actually provide stable income so you  look at some of the Western European  countries that have instead of  furloughing workers or laying off  workers have kept workers up pretty much  on the job and employed through  government payments I think you need a  you need a an assumption of a social  welfare system to pass that kind of  legislation in an emergency context  which is something we of course don't  have here are there other countries what  other countries are at the top of the  list in terms of dealing with the  medical part I mean what's happened in  South Korea is incredible they still  don't have 300 deaths deaths they don't  least they're under 300 deaths which is  just stunning given that their first  case was officially found within hours  of the first u.s. case Germany has done  well although they had a far larger  outbreak and and I think it was just a  little more advanced by the time they  got around to it there's a lot of debate  about what's happening in Sweden  certainly a higher death rate now but  you're sort of taking a bet that maybe  the subsequent waves won't be as deadly  because you'll have higher immunity we  just don't know my view is that we just  won't know that for a while what are the  other countries that medically have done  the best yeah and one thing I would  agree with you about the uncertainty  about the outcome in a place like Sweden  but one thing I would say is that  insofar as a  is being made that we're kind of  accidentally following the Swedish model  in Sweden they have made sure that the  health systems are strong and that  people you know will have available  medical care so it's one thing to to let  things go without a plan in place and  it's another thing to sort of carefully  keep things less shut down than  elsewhere when you know that you're  gonna be able to provide serious medical  care for those who do get sick I think  the other countries to look at very  carefully although of course they're  different situations than the United  States our East Asian countries so  Taiwan Singapore places that experienced  stars were very proactive early on in in  January putting in place testing systems  contact tracing systems isolation  practices in some cases closing down  schools and businesses early in another  case in other cases not but it looks  like they those countries have for the  most part weather this much better than  the United States has one of the ideas  that's floated about when we look at  some of these other countries is that we  can't really do what some of those  countries do because they are far more  authoritarian now with the case of South  Korea South Korea ranks higher than the  United States on the democracy index  only slightly so but but they do and  certainly to say that South Korea  handled it because they're an  authoritarian system of government seems  completely false when it comes to some  other places  is there anything to the idea that not  all of these measures would be tolerable  in the United States politically well I  think you know again just to add to your  point a place like Germany there's no  way you could make an argument that  contemporary Germany is more  authoritarian than the United States  yeah but it really has to do with  competency trust and expertise proactive  government intervention and trust by the  public in that intervention so it's not  so much a question of authoritarian  versus democratic but when you have a  when you have a liberal democratic  country what kind of system do you need  to have in place which of course we had  we had planned for such a system here  that wouldn't invade fundamental rights  the problem is we didn't implement that  plan last thing I want to touch on going  forward there may be a second  and third wave there may just be a  second one this first wave may never  really diminish and it may sort of go  into the time where we would potentially  have a second wave we don't know the  timeline for a wide widely available  vaccine we don't exactly know when any  of the 70 or 80 possible treatments may  turn up to work very effectively there's  economic concerns we still aren't  testing enough what are you looking at  and what would be the the lowest hanging  fruit to do over the next six months  well I think what we what we're  obviously going to have a period of  trial and error over the next six months  as we figure out what works and what  doesn't work because obviously we won't  have a vaccine in that period or very  it's very unlikely we would I think what  we need what what I'm looking at is just  the extent to which we're not flying  blind as we go forward how much are we  actually tracking what's going on and  proactively intervening before things  get bad again each time we try to lessen  our social distancing measures so the  more information we have the better and  the more that information ties into  actual rapid policy decisions the better  outcome we'll have are the over the next  few months so it sounds like at an  absolute minimum  no matter what the trajectory is going  to be if we don't have dramatically more  testing we are going to be short on the  information we need to really make  informed decisions I think that's right  we need more we need a dramatically more  testing but we also need to new to know  what we're going to do with the results  of those tests we actually need a plan  that links tests to policies so it's not  just the testing but also acceptance of  guidelines from from a central  coordinated government effort that will  lead to less bad outcomes we've been  speaking with Andrew lay cough  sociologist at the University of  Southern California also author of the  book unprepared global health in a time  of emergency Andrew I really appreciate  your time today thanks for having me  here

